  "findings": [
    "Paced trace exposes an XPC/VideoToolbox pixel-buffer-receive dispatch worker with substantial blocked intervals between short running bursts. Blocked duration includes idle time and cannot be treated as time waiting for the hardware decode of a frame.",
    "Target Runnable intervals are directly observable, but without per-frame alignment cannot explain the decode interval or attribute the cadence gap to priority/descheduling.",
    "Paced sampled stacks include CoreMedia XPC pixel-buffer reconstruction, IOSurface, and CVPixelBuffer attachment work. These are concrete receive-path work candidates, not measured per-frame bottlenecks.",
    "The throughput export contains no target CPU samples; compare neither sample shares nor scheduling coverage as if both captures were complete."
  ],
  "limits": [
    "System Trace reports Windowed (5 seconds) for both captures; elapsed target duration does not guarantee continuous event or stack coverage. Actual exported target ranges and row counts are retained.",
    "Time-profile is statistical 1ms sampling of running threads only. It does not measure hardware engine execution or blocked stacks continuously.",
    "No main submit-side API stack appears in the sampled target frames. No new claim about inside-VT waits or causal priority behavior is supported.",
    "Raw exports include system metadata locally; this evidence embeds only the target process and relevant library stack names, not a personal process inventory.",
    "Instrumented replay timings are diagnostic only and must not be pooled into uninstrumented performance results. One capture per mode is not a repeated controlled comparison.",
    "Thread-state duration totals are per-thread sums, not elapsed wall time; Unknown, Terminated and Idle intervals remain separate.",
    "Syscall CPU/wait columns are not interpreted as an additive latency decomposition; some exported values exceed the enclosing call interval.",
    "Throughput syscall export was omitted because absence of target CPU samples and bounded investigation time prevent a useful causal comparison."
  ],
  "recommendation": "Use an explicit shared-clock/signpost anchor around submit and callback, and verify target-thread/event coverage in a new diagnostic capture before assigning the cadence gap to scheduling or IPC; no production QoS change is justified by these captures."
